      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate markdown carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01 revision 6\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 4086814\n- Page total: 7\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 10 August 2026\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Address: Thijseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, the Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Title: Certification Board (the signing entity is \"Certification Board\")\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Euromag International S.r.l., Via Balla, 55, 35010 Villafranca Padovana (PD), Italy\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant: Euromag International S.r.l. (labeled \"Applicant and Manufacturer\")\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: Water meter (electromagnetic water meter)\n- Type designations: MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300, MC406M, MC406AM\n- Description: An electromagnetic water meter, Type: MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL and MUT2300 with electronic converter MC406M and MC406AM\n\n**Components:**\n- Measuring sensor: MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300\n- Electronic converter (indicating device/calculator): MC406M, MC406AM\n\nActually the sensor types are the MUT types and the converters are MC406M/MC406AM. Let me record components:\n- role: \"measuring sensor\", type_designations: [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\"]\n- role: \"electronic converter\", type_designations: [\"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"]\n\nHmm, but MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL are full bore and MUT2300 reduced bore. The certificate identifies the type as the water meter with converters.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- R49-1 (2013) → id: \"R49-1\" — hmm, canonical form. The recommendation is R49-1: 2013 \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\". Canonical id: \"R49-1\" (or \"R49\"?). The certificate says \"R49-1 (2013)\". I'll use \"R49-1\" with edition 2013. Edition as int: 2013? The schema says edition (int). For OIML R49-1 (2013), the edition is 2013. Amendment: null.\n- scheme: A\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2] — from \"Accuracy class 1 and 2\". But note the type-specific classes: MUT2200EL class 2, MUT2300 class 1. Overall: [1, 2].\n\n**Test reports:**\n- NMi-15200444-01, dated 31 March 2016, 39 pages\n- NMi-16200309-01 revision 1, dated 22 March 2018, 29 pages\n- NMi-1902198-01, dated 3 December 2018, 16 pages\n- NMi-2186686-01, dated 24 May 2019, 43 pages\n- NMi-2186686-02, dated 24 May 2019, 47 pages\n- NMi-2463352-01, dated 18 January 2021, 27 pages\n- NMi-3369067-01, dated 10 May 2022, 13 pages\n- NMi-3670720-01, dated 22 September 2023, 12 pages\n- NMi-4086814-01, dated 10 August 2026, 21 pages\n\nRole: type_evaluation_report for all (they are the associated reports). Hmm, they say \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". Role: \"type_evaluation_report\" seems reasonable.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- Initial, 24 May 2019, \"-\" (no changes)\n- 1, 4 February 2021, \"Addition of MUT1000EL and full-bore sizes DN32 and DN40; Lowering pressure loss class of MUT2300\"\n- 2, 10 May 2022, \"Addition of electronic hardware module (Bluetooth&RS485 and/or 4-20mA output). Addition of new software.\"\n- 3, 7 July 2022, \"Software update\"\n- 4, 14 February 2023, \"Software update\"\n- 5, 22 September 2023, \"Update of electronic hardware module. Software update\"\n- 6, 10 August 2026, \"Address change, addition of new sizes, increase of Q3 values, addition of new software and minor hardware change.\"\n\n**Model family:**\nThe cert covers multiple types: MUT1000EL (full bore, no flanges), MUT2200EL (full bore, with flanges), MUT2300 (reduced bore). Plus meter sizes DN32–DN600. The most natural modeling: models = meter sizes per type family. There are two tables: full bore (Table 2, 16 sizes: DN32..DN450 + DN500, DN600) and reduced bore (Table 3, 14 sizes DN50..DN600).\n\nModel IDs: I could use \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\" etc. But that's 30 models. Alternatively, model_id per size within each family table. The schema example uses model_id like \"G1.6\". Here the model variants are meter sizes combined with type.\n\nApproach: model_family with models representing each type+size combination? That could be verbose but thorough. Or use config_level with axis \"meter_size\"? Hmm.\n\nThe R137 example shows model_family.models = [{\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"}, ...] and matrix_tables for the 2-D table, plus model_level characteristics for varying attributes.\n\nHere we have two separate tables (full bore and reduced bore), each with rows = meter sizes and columns = Ø, Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q3/Q1. I'll emit two matrix_tables and model_level characteristics with model ids like \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\"... that's long. Maybe model_id = \"DN32 (full bore)\" and \"DN50 (reduced bore)\"? Since sizes overlap (DN50..DN600 appear in both), need to distinguish.\n\nBetter: model_id like \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN50\" vs \"MUT2300 DN50\". Or simpler: \"Full bore DN50\" and \"Reduced bore DN50\".\n\nHmm, but the actual distinct models are MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300, and within each, multiple sizes. Let me think about what's most useful for downstream.\n\nOption A: models = 30 entries (16 full bore + 14 reduced bore), model_id e.g. \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\".\n\nActually full bore types are \"MUT1000EL (without flanges) or MUT2200EL (with flanges)\" — same characteristics. So the full bore family shares sizes. DN32 and DN40 were added in rev 1 as full-bore sizes (MUT1000EL and MUT2200EL).\n\nI'll go with model_id: \"Full bore DN32\", ..., \"Full bore DN600\", \"Reduced bore DN50\", ..., \"Reduced bore DN600\". And include attributes in each model entry? The schema models[] entries have {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I could put the attributes directly in each model (Ø, Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio). That duplicates matrix_tables but that's fine per the R137 example which populated both matrix_tables AND model_level.\n\nGiven the size, maybe I'll put attributes in models[] entries AND provide matrix_tables, and skip model_level to avoid triple duplication? The rules say \"populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\" in the R137 example, along with matrix_tables. The example shows both. To follow instructions, I should do model_level too. But that's a lot of JSON. Let me count: 30 models × 6 attributes. In model_level format that's 6 attribute entries each with 30 values. Plus matrix_tables rows 16 + 14. Plus models[] 30 entries.\n\nHmm, alternatively models[] entries with attributes{} could be compact:\n\n```json\n{\"model_id\": \"Full bore DN32\", \"attributes\": {\"diameter\": 32, \"q1\": 0.125, ...}}\n```\n\nThat's fine. And model_level for the key varying attributes. Actually, to be thorough but not insanely redundant, I'll do:\n- matrix_tables: two tables (full name \"General characteristics of the family of instruments – Full bore type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL\" and reduced bore).\n- model_family.models: 30 entries with attributes.\n- model_level: maybe skip? The rules say matrix detection is most important and to populate model_level. The R137 example populates model_level with qmax, qt, qmin only (not all columns). So I can do a subset: the flow rates q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, and diameter. Let me do all: bore_diameter, minimum_flow_rate_q1, transitional_flow_rate_q2, permanent_flow_rate_q3, overload_flow_rate_q4, ratio_q3_q1. 6 attributes × 30 values = 180 value objects. That's big but acceptable.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe I should be careful about output length. Let me estimate: each value object ~50-70 chars. 180 × 65 ≈ 12k chars. matrix_tables rows: 30 rows × ~120 chars ≈ 4k. models: 30 × ~150 ≈ 4.5k. Total ~20k chars for the family. That's fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: do I need BOTH models[] with attributes AND model_level? The schema says model_level[]: attribute × model matrix. To be safe and consistent with the example, include both. But maybe models[] entries don't need attributes{} if model_level covers it — the example R137 models entries are just {\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"} with no attributes, and model_level carries the values. So follow that: models[] with just model_id (and label for the type), and model_level with the varying attributes, matrix_tables with the full tables.\n\nThat matches the example best. So:\n\nmodels: [{\"model_id\": \"Full bore DN32\", \"label\": \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\"}, ...]\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider the model_id naming. The certificate type designations are MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL, MUT2300. Sizes are variants. For MUT1000EL vs MUT2200EL, characteristics are identical (Table 2 covers both). So model per (type-family, size):\n\n- \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\" ... DN600 (16 sizes)\n- \"MUT2300 DN50\" ... DN600 (14 sizes)\n\nModel ids with \"/\" might be awkward. Alternative: \"MUT1000EL-MUT2200EL DN32\". I'll use model_id: \"DN32 (full bore)\" style? Hmm. Let me just pick: model_id: \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\", label: \"Full bore DN32\". Or model_id: \"Full bore DN32\", label: \"MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL, DN32\". \n\nI think cleaner: model_id: \"DN32-full_bore\" ... hmm. Let me go with \"Full bore DN32\" and \"Reduced bore DN50\" as model_ids, with label giving the type names: \"MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL, DN32\" and \"MUT2300, DN50\".\n\nActually wait — should accuracy class be per model? Table 1: Accuracy class of MUT2200EL = 2, MUT2300 = 1. What about MUT1000EL? Not explicitly given. Interesting. The certificate says accuracy class 1 and 2. MUT1000EL accuracy class isn't stated separately. Hmm. I'll add model_level accuracy_class for MUT2200EL (=2) and MUT2300 (=1)? But model ids are size-based... The accuracy class varies by type, not size. Could use type_level for... no, it's two different values. I could put it in model_level with model = \"MUT2200EL\" (all sizes) and \"MUT2300\" (all sizes). That's a slight mismatch in model naming but conveys the info. Alternatively put in characteristics.type_level? No — type_level must be single value.\n\nBetter: put accuracy class as an attribute in config_level? Axis \"type\": values: [{condition: \"MUT2200EL (full bore)\", value: 2}, {condition: \"MUT2300 (reduced bore)\", value: 1}]. Hmm, config_level is for configuration axes. Type isn't exactly config.\n\nAlternatively, just include accuracy_class in each model's attributes in models[] entries. If I include attributes in models[], I can capture accuracy_class per model cleanly. But then I'm duplicating again.\n\nLet me simplify: models[] entries will include attributes{accuracy_class, measuring_tube, ...} minimal, and model_level carries flow characteristics. Actually, hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider — maybe cleanest: model_level entries:\n- accuracy_class: values for MUT2200EL full bore models = 2, MUT2300 models = 1. Since model ids are per type+size, I can list each model with its class. 30 values. Fine, just do it.\n\nActually MUT1000EL: accuracy class unknown. For full bore models, MUT2200EL has class 2. MUT1000EL's class isn't stated. I could give full bore models accuracy_class 2 with a note? Rule 8: don't fabricate. Table 1 says \"Accuracy class of MUT2200EL: 2\". The full bore family is MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL. So for model \"Full bore DN50\", the accuracy class is 2 (via MUT2200EL). I think it's safe to attribute class 2 to full-bore models since Table 2 covers MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL and Table 1 assigns class 2 to MUT2200EL. Hmm, but strictly it's for MUT2200EL. To be safe I'll note it via model_level with model \"Full bore DNxx\" value 2 — and perhaps add a footnote? There are no footnotes in the doc. \n\nAlternative approach: keep accuracy class out of model_level and instead record in characteristics.type_level? No, can't — two values.\n\nI could use config_level with axis \"converter_version\"? No.\n\nSimplest defensible: model_level entry attribute \"accuracy_class\", values: for each of the 16 full-bore models value 2 (attribute to MUT2200EL), for each of 14 MUT2300 models value 1. And mention in the matrix_tables description that Table 1 states MUT2200EL class 2, MUT2300 class 1.\n\nHmm wait, actually — maybe better to handle accuracy class at the \"type\" level differently: record it in certified_type.description or as separate model-level for just the three type designations rather than 30 size models. I'll include it in model_level keyed to the 30 models as decided. Fine.\n\n**Type-level characteristics (Table 1 + others):**\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (string value)\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" — string. Could split: environmental_class \"M1/O\", mounting \"installed outdoors\". I'll keep as one string value \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\". Or value: \"M1/O\", with separate note. Rules: structured values separate numeric from unit — this is a class code, keep string. I'll do value: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\".\n- electromagnetic_environment: varies by converter version → config_level! Axis: \"converter_version\". Values:\n  - {condition: \"MC406M remote version\", value: \"E1\"}\n  - {condition: \"MC406M compact version\", value: \"E2\"}\n  - {condition: \"MC406AM compact and remote version\", value: \"E2\"}\n  - {condition: \"MC406M/AM compact and remote version with Bluetooth&RS485 and/or 4-20mA output\", value: \"E1\"}\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit \"°C\"\n- water_temperature_class: T50 (+0.1°C / +50°C) → water_temperature_class: \"T50\", and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. The \"+0,1 °C\" is odd (likely means 0.1°C to 50°C for T50 cold potable... actually T50 is 0.1–50°C). I'll record: water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}, water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 1.6 MPa (16 bar) → value: 1.6, unit: \"MPa\", plus separate map_bar: 16, unit \"bar\"? Or one value with unit \"MPa\" and alt in parentheses. I'll do maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa and add a second entry maximum_admissible_pressure_alt: 16 bar. Or just the MPa one with the bar as footnote... Keep both as separate characteristics.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\" — string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" — string.\n- reverse_flow: \"The water meter is designed to measure reverse flow\" → value: true? Or string. I'll do value: \"designed to measure reverse flow\" or boolean true. Boolean true with attribute reverse_flow_measurement. Hmm, keep as string to be safe? A boolean is clean: {\"value\": true}. The schema allows number | {min,max} | string. Not boolean explicitly. Use string \"measures reverse flow\" — attribute reverse_flow, value: \"designed to measure reverse flow\". OK.\n- pressure_loss_class full bore: \"Δp 10 for all sizes\" → config_level or type_level string: pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp10\", condition?}. Type_level: attribute pressure_loss_class, value \"Δp 10 for all sizes\"? Better: config_level axis \"type and size\". Let me structure:\n  - Full bore (MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL): Δp 10, all sizes. Based on documentation 10713/6-01, 10713/6-02, 10713/12-01.\n  - MUT2300 per documentation 10713/1-01: Δp 25 for sizes < DN80; Δp 40 for sizes ≥ DN80.\n  - MUT2300 per documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03: for sizes < DN250 see table; for sizes ≥ DN250: Δp 40 (0.040 MPa or 0.40 bar).\n\n  I'll capture via config_level: attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"type_and_size\", values with conditions. And matrix_tables entry for the DN50–DN200 pressure loss table with alternative classes.\n\n- power_supply: battery (2.9–3.7 V) and DC mains (10–28 V) only for MC406AM. → config_level: attribute \"power_supply_voltage\", axis \"power_source\":\n  - {condition: \"replaceable battery\", value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit \"V\"}\n  - {condition: \"DC mains (MC406AM only)\", value: {min: 10, max: 28}, unit \"V\"}\n  Or two type_level entries: battery_voltage range and mains_voltage range. I'll use config_level with axis \"power_source\".\n\n- software identification: Bootloader versions+CRC table; Legally relevant firmware versions+CRC table. → two matrix_tables: \"Software identification – Bootloader\" and \"Software identification – Legally relevant firmware\".\n\nNote: Bootloader has \"01.02\" twice with different CRCs — preserve both rows.\n\n**Matrix tables:**\n1. Table 2 – full bore: columns: meter_size, diameter_inlet_outlet (mm), q1 (m³/h), q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. 16 rows.\n2. Table 3 – reduced bore: 14 rows.\n3. Pressure loss class table (MUT2300, sizes < DN250): columns: meter_size, pressure_loss_class, alternative_pressure_loss_class. 7 rows.\n4. Software tables (2).\n5. Tables 4 & 5 – indicating device characteristics: columns meter_size, indicating_range_minimum_value (m³), verification_scale_interval (m³). Two tables (full bore: 6 grouped rows; reduced bore: 6 grouped rows).\n\nShould I make Tables 4/5 into matrix_tables? Yes, they're multi-dim tables with grouped sizes. The rows group multiple sizes (e.g., \"DN40, DN50\"). I can keep the grouped cell as string value for meter_size, or expand. I'll keep grouped strings in matrix_tables but expand into model_level? That'd be messy. Keep matrix_tables with grouped size strings, and optionally model_level entries for indicating_range and verification_scale_interval with per-model values. Hmm, that's another 2×30 entries. Let me include them — it's valuable data. Verification scale interval per size: full bore DN32: 0.0001; DN40,DN50: 0.001; DN65–DN100: 0.001; DN125,DN150: 0.01; DN200–DN300: 0.01; DN350–DN600: 0.1. Reduced: DN50: 0.0001; DN65: 0.001; DN80–DN150: 0.001; DN200: 0.01; DN250–DN400: 0.01; DN450–DN600: 0.1.\n\nThat's 30 values × 2 attributes = 60 more value objects. Output is getting large but let me just do it. Actually, hmm. Total size estimate: let me be a bit economical. I'll include model_level for: bore diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval. That's 9 attributes × 30 = 270 value objects. Each ~55 chars → ~15k chars. Plus matrix_tables duplicating (Tables 2,3 full + 4,5 + pressure loss + 2 software tables) ~8k. Plus models[] ~2k. Plus the rest ~4k. Total ~30k chars JSON. That should be OK.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe I should reconsider whether to include attributes in models[] — I'll keep models[] light: model_id + label only (like the R137 example). \n\nActually, let me reconsider the overall size. The instructions emphasize matrix detection. I'll do it properly.\n\nLet me now fix decimal conversions: 0,125 → 0.125; 78,75 → 78.75; 312,5 → 312.5; etc. All European commas → dots.\n\n**Full bore Table 2 rows (converting):**\n- DN32: Ø32, Q1 0.125, Q2 0.2, Q3 25, Q4 31.25, ratio 200\n- DN40: 40, 0.2, 0.32, 40, 50, 200\n- DN50: 50, 0.315, 0.504, 63, 78.75, 200\n- DN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN100: 100, 1.25, 2, 250, 312.5, 200\n- DN125: 125, 2, 3.2, 400, 500, 200\n- DN150: 150, 3.15, 5.04, 630, 787.5, 200\n- DN200: 200, 5, 8, 1000, 1250, 200\n- DN250: 250, 8, 12.8, 1600, 2000, 200\n- DN300: 300, 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n- DN350: 350, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN400: 400, 20, 32, 4000, 5000, 200\n- DN450: 450, 31.5, 50.4, 6300, 7875, 200\n- DN500: 500, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN600: 600, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n\n**Reduced bore Table 3 rows:**\n- DN50: 50, 0.2, 0.32, 40, 50, 315\n- DN65: 65, 0.315, 0.504, 63, 78.75, 315\n- DN80: 80, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 315\n- DN100: 100, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 315\n- DN125: 125, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 315\n- DN150: 150, 2, 3.2, 400, 500, 315\n- DN200: 200, 3.15, 5.04, 630, 787.5, 315\n- DN250: 250, 5, 8, 1000, 1250, 200\n- DN300: 300, 8, 12.8, 1600, 2000, 200\n- DN350: 350, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN400: 400, 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n- DN450: 450, 20, 32, 4000, 5000, 200\n- DN500: 500, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n- DN600: 600, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n\n**Accuracy class per model:**\n- Full bore models (MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL): Table 1 gives class for MUT2200EL = 2. I'll assign 2 to full bore models, noting it's per MUT2200EL. For MUT1000EL, not stated — but they share the table. I'll set 2 for full bore and 1 for reduced bore (MUT2300). Include a footnote? No footnotes exist; I'll describe in matrix_tables description.\n\nHmm, actually is it safe? The overall cert says accuracy classes 1 and 2. MUT2300=1, MUT2200EL=2. Full bore covers MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL. MUT1000EL is physically MUT2200EL without flanges presumably. I'll assign 2 to full bore. Reasonable.\n\n**Model list naming:** I'll use:\n- Full bore: model_id \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\" ... \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN600\"; label \"Full bore DN32\" etc.\n- Reduced: model_id \"MUT2300 DN50\" ... ; label \"Reduced bore DN50\".\n\nModel_id with \"/\" is fine in JSON.\n\nActually hmm, simpler: model_id \"Full bore DN32\" etc. But then the link to type designations is via label. I think including type in model_id is more informative: \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\". Let me use that. Hmm, it makes model_level values bulky (30 × \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DNxxx\"). Alternatively use short ids \"FB-DN32\" and \"RB-DN50\" with labels explaining. That reduces size a lot. E.g. model_id: \"FB-DN32\", label: \"MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL (full bore), DN32\". Hmm, but readability for downstream... The example used \"G1.6\" directly. \n\nI'll go with \"FB DN32\"... hmm, \"FB\" cryptic. Let me use \"Full bore DN32\" / \"Reduced bore DN50\" as model_id, with label containing the type: \"MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL, DN32, full bore\". That's a good balance. Actually model_id should ideally be stable identifier. \"Full bore DN32\" is fine.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: maybe simply have models be