cating range (minimum value)\" means the minimum value of the indicating range? Actually it's likely the maximum indicated value (99999 m³ etc.). The header says \"Indicating range (minimum value)\" — probably the range's upper limit... ambiguous. Keep attribute as given: \"indicating_range_minimum_value\". Value numeric, unit m³.\n\n**Model level entries:**\n\n1. bore_diameter (Ø in- and outlet): unit \"mm\" — 30 values.\n2. minimum_flow_rate_q1: unit \"m³/h\" — 30 values.\n3. transitional_flow_rate_q2: unit \"m³/h\" — 30 values.\n4. permanent_flow_rate_q3: unit \"m³/h\" — 30 values.\n5. overload_flow_rate_q4: unit \"m³/h\" — 30 values.\n6. ratio_q3_q1: unit null — 30 values.\n7. accuracy_class: unit null — 30 values.\n8. indicating_range_minimum_value: unit \"m³\" — 30 values.\n9. verification_scale_interval: unit \"m³\" — 30 values.\n\nThat's a lot of repetition but it's what the schema asks. Let me write it compactly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider #7–#9: maybe just do q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio, diameter in model_level, and leave accuracy_class + indicating device stuff in matrix_tables + models[].attributes. Hmm.\n\nThe R137 example put only qmax, qt, qmin in model_level (not cyclic_volume, max_pressure_loss, pmax which were in the matrix table). So the precedent allows a subset. But being thorough is better for extraction quality. I'll include all 9 — the JSON will be large but complete. Actually... let me estimate more carefully.\n\nEach value entry like {\"model\": \"Full bore DN32\", \"value\": 0.125} ≈ 45 chars. 270 entries × 45 = ~12k chars. Acceptable.\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me reconsider using shorter model_ids to reduce bloat: \"FB-DN32\" style. Full bore = MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL; reduced = MUT2300. I could define in model_family a note... there's no notes field, but label covers it. model_id: \"FB DN32\"? I'll use \"FB-DN32\" and \"RB-DN50\" with labels. Hmm, but \"Full bore DN32\" is only 8 chars longer. Use \"Full bore DN32\". Fine.\n\n**Type-level characteristics final list:**\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" — maybe split: environmental_class \"M1/O\", installation \"outdoors\". I'll keep single string.\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min:-25, max:55}, \"°C\"\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min:0.1, max:50}, \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, \"MPa\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16, \"bar\" — hmm, naming. Or include in same? I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt\" value 16 unit \"bar\". Or skip as noise? It's informative. Keep it.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure reverse flow\"\n\n**config_level:**\n1. electromagnetic_environment (axis: converter_version_and_configuration) — 4 conditions.\n2. pressure_loss_class (axis: type_and_size) — 5 conditions.\n3. power_supply_voltage (axis: power_source) — 2 conditions.\n\n**Components:** \n- role: \"measuring sensor (electromagnetic water meter)\", type_designations: [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\"], characteristics maybe: measuring_tube variants. Add sub info: MUT1000EL full bore without flanges; MUT2200EL full bore with flanges; MUT2300 reduced bore. Put in \"description\" or characteristics. I'll add \"description\" field to components? Schema components: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I can put characteristics: {\"measuring_tube\": ...}? Keep simple:\n  - {role: \"measuring sensor\", type_designations: [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\"], characteristics: {\"tube_variants\": ...}} — hmm characteristics values should be structured? Not strictly specified for components. I'll put description-ish strings. Actually maybe better to put the flange info in certified_type.description text. Components:\n  - {role: \"measuring sensor\", type_designations: [\"MUT1000EL (full bore, without flanges)\", \"MUT2200EL (full bore, with flanges)\", \"MUT2300 (reduced bore)\"]} — or keep designations clean and add characteristics: {\"measuring_tube\": \"cylindrical; full bore (MUT1000EL without flanges, MUT2200EL with flanges) or reduced bore (MUT2300)\"}. Good.\n  - {role: \"electronic converter (calculator/indicating device)\", type_designations: [\"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"]}\n\n**Recommendation:** id \"R49-1\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [1, 2]. Hmm — canonical: rules example said R60 not \"OIML R 60\". So \"R49-1\". Hmm, or \"R49\"? The cert title: R49-1 (2013). Use \"R49-1\". Amendment: null. Hmm, should scheme be at recommendation level? Schema shows recommendation.scheme. Include \"A\".\n\nWait, actually — accuracy_classes at recommendation level: [1, 2].\n\n**Certificate:** number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\", revision handling: schema certificate has number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. Revision: the number includes \"revision 6\". I'll set number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\" and... there's no revision field in certificate schema. Revision history captures revisions. Maybe include revision in number string: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01 revision 6\"? Cleaner: number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\" and add \"revision\": \"6\"? Rule 8: don't fabricate fields not in schema? Adding extra field is probably fine but schema-adherence matters. The revision_history has revision \"6\" date \"10 August 2026\" which matches date_issued. I'll include number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\" and add \"revision\": \"6\" as an extra field — hmm. The schema for certificate doesn't list revision, but revision_history covers it. I'll add it anyway? Risky vs helpful. I'll include it — extra keys are usually tolerated, and it preserves info. Actually, revision_history already has it. But tying cert number to revision is useful. I'll add \"revision\": \"6\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2026-10-08\"? Or keep \"10 August 2026\". ISO format is safer for machine use. The schema doesn't specify format. I'll use ISO \"2026-10-08\". For test report dates too: \"2016-03-31\", etc. Revision history dates ISO as well: \"2019-05-24\", \"2021-02-04\", \"2022-05-10\", \"2022-07-07\", \"2023-02-14\", \"2023-09-22\", \"2026-08-10\".\n\n**Test reports:**\n- {id: \"NMi-15200444-01\", date: \"2016-03-31\", pages: 39, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-16200309-01\", date: \"2018-03-22\", pages: 29, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — note \"revision 1\"; add revision field? Schema: id, date, pages, role. Add \"revision\": \"1\" extra? I'll include revision: 1 maybe. Hmm, keep extra field minimal. I'll add \"revision\": \"1\" — helpful. Or skip. I'll include it since it's factual.\n\nActually to stay strict, maybe fold into id: \"NMi-16200309-01 rev. 1\"? No, better separate field. I'll add \"revision\": \"1\".\n\n- NMi-1902198-01, 2018-12-03, 16\n- NMi-2186686-01, 2019-05-24, 43\n- NMi-2186686-02, 2019-05-24, 47\n- NMi-2463352-01, 2021-01-18, 27\n- NMi-3369067-01, 2022-05-10, 13\n- NMi-3670720-01, 2023-09-22, 12\n- NMi-4086814-01, 2026-08-10, 21\n\nRole: \"type_evaluation_report\" for all (they're the Type Evaluation Reports / associated reports). The doc says \"associated reports\" and \"Documentation folder no. T10713-10\" — documentation_file. Add test_reports entry: {id: \"T10713-10\", role: \"documentation_file\"}? It says \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T10713-10.\" Yes, add as documentation_file. No date/pages.\n\n**Footnotes:** none in the document. Empty array or omit. I'll omit (or include empty? Rule 8: omit if not present). Omit.\n\n**Issuing authority details:**\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\" — also \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\". oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n- address_lines: [\"Thijseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"the Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: \"Certification Board\" — the signature block says \"Certification Board\" under the authority/date. It's plausibly the title/role of the signer. Include person_title: \"Certification Board\".\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n\n**Certificate date_issued:** 10 August 2026 → \"2026-08-10\".\n\n**certified_type:**\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\", \"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"]\n- module_designation: null? For water meters there isn't an analog \"module designation\" like load cells. Omit or null. I'll set null.\n- description: \"Electromagnetic water meter, types MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL and MUT2300, with electronic converter MC406M or MC406AM. Measuring sensor with cylindrical measuring tube: full bore (MUT1000EL without flanges or MUT2200EL with flanges) or reduced bore (MUT2300).\"\n\n**Model family:**\n- family_name: null (no explicit family name; tables say \"family of instruments\" — could set family_name: null). \n- models: 30 entries.\n\nLet me now write out the model ids:\nFull bore (16): Full bore DN32, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\nReduced bore (14): Reduced bore DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\n\nLabels: \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\" and \"MUT2300 DN50\".\n\nModel_level entries — let me build the values arrays.\n\naccuracy_class: full bore → 2, reduced → 1.\n\nNow writing everything. Also matrix_tables: I'll include:\n1. \"general_characteristics_full_bore\" — columns: meter_size, diameter_inlet_outlet, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1; column_units: [null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\", null]; rows 16.\n2. \"general_characteristics_reduced_bore\" — rows 14.\n3. \"pressure_loss_class_mut2300\" — columns: meter_size, pressure_loss_class, alternative_pressure_loss_class; rows 7.\n4. \"software_versions_bootloader\" — columns: software_version, crc_checksum; rows 4.\n5. \"software_versions_legally_relevant_firmware\" — rows 10.\n6. \"indicating_device_full_bore\" — columns: meter_size, indicating_range_minimum_value, verification_scale_interval; units [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"]; rows 6.\n7. \"indicating_device_reduced_bore\" — rows 6.\n\nmatrix_tables name field — free text names. Use readable names: \"Table 2 – General characteristics of the family of instruments – Full bore type MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL\" etc.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nNow config_level details:\n\nelectromagnetic_environment:\n- condition: \"MC406M, remote version\", value \"E1\"\n- condition: \"MC406M, compact version\", value \"E2\"\n- condition: \"MC406AM, compact and remote version\", value \"E2\"\n- condition: \"MC406M/MC406AM, compact and remote version, with Bluetooth&RS485 and/or 4-20mA output\", value \"E1\"\n\nvalue shape for config_level: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The schema says values[]: {condition, value}. Structured form: value as string \"E1\" is fine (plain string, not object). In model_level example values are {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}. For config_level: {\"condition\": \"...\", \"value\": \"E1\"}. Should I add unit_symbol? Only when unit exists. For class codes, none.\n\npressure_loss_class config values:\n- {condition: \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL (full bore), all sizes (per documentation 10713/6-01, 10713/6-02, 10713/12-01)\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n- {condition: \"MUT2300 (reduced bore), sizes < DN80 (per documentation 10713/1-01)\", value: \"Δp 25\"}\n- {condition: \"MUT2300 (reduced bore), sizes ≥ DN80 (per documentation 10713/1-01)\", value: \"Δp 40\"}\n- {condition: \"MUT2300 (reduced bore), sizes < DN250 (per documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03)\", value: \"see pressure loss table\"}\n- {condition: \"MUT2300 (reduced bore), sizes ≥ DN250 (per documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03)\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.040 MPa or 0.40 bar)\"}\n\npower_supply_voltage:\n- {condition: \"replaceable battery\", value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- {condition: \"DC mains (only for MC406AM)\", value: {min: 10, max: 28}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n\nAlso maybe power_supply_type: battery/DC mains — covered by conditions. Fine.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n\n\"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic\"}\n\"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\"} — hmm, or environmental_class \"M1/O\" + \"installation\": \"outdoors\". Keep single: {\"value\": \"M1/O\", ...} with note? I'll do {\"value\": \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\"}. Slightly messy but faithful.\n\"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\"} — unit_id: the rule says leave null unless certain. \"u:degree_Celsius\" was given as example in schema so it's presumably right. I'll set it for °C. For m³/h, MPa etc. I'm not certain of unitsdb ids → null.\n\"water_temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\"}\n\"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\"}\n\"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\"}\n\"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"} — hmm, attribute naming: \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\"? I'll name it \"maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent\". Eh. Or just include parenthetical in first: value 1.6 MPa. And add second characteristic. I'll do:\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\"},\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}\n\"orientation\": {\"value\": \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"}\n\"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n\"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": \"designed to measure reverse flow\"}\n\nAlso flow ratio selection notes (Q2/Q1=1.6, Q4/Q3=1.25, Q3/Q1 ≥ 40) — could add type_level:\n- \"ratio_q2_q1\": {\"value\": 1.6}\n- \"ratio_q4_q3\": {\"value\": 1.25}\n- \"minimum_ratio_q3_q1\": {\"value\": 40}\nWith note they're freely chosen within limits. These are from the \"Please note\" sections. Good to include.\n\nAlso flow rate selection: \"Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013(E)\" — skip as text.\n\nNow — the models array. Should each model include attributes? I decided no (matching example). But then accuracy_class at model level: include as model_level entry with 30 values. OK.\n\nLet me also double check reduced bore DN125: Q1 0.8, Q2 1.28 — same as DN100. Yes per table.\n\nAlso note Table 2 spans pages 4–5 (DN500, DN600 on page 5). Combined.\n\nNow the indicating device model_level:\nindicating_range_minimum_value (m³):\nFull bore: DN32: 99999; DN40: 99999; DN50: 99999; DN65: 999999; DN80: 999999; DN100: 999999; DN125: 999999; DN150: 999999; DN200: 9999999; DN250: 9999999; DN300: 9999999; DN350: 9999999; DN400: 9999999; DN450: 9999999; DN500: 9999999; DN600: 9999999.\nReduced: DN50: 99999; DN65: 99999; DN80: 999999; DN100: 999999; DN125: 999999; DN150: 999999; DN200: 999999; DN250: 9999999; DN300: 9999999; DN350: 9999999; DN400: 9999999; DN450: 9999999; DN500: 9999999; DN600: 9999999.\n\nverification_scale_interval (m³):\nFull: DN32: 0.0001; DN40: 0.001; DN50: 0.001; DN65: 0.001; DN80: 0.001; DN100: 0.001; DN125: 0.01; DN150: 0.01; DN200: 0.01; DN250: 0.01; DN300: 0.01; DN350: 0.1; DN400: 0.1; DN450: 0.1; DN500: 0.1; DN600: 0.1.\nReduced: DN50: 0.0001; DN65: 0.001; DN80: 0.001; DN100: 0.001; DN125: 0.001; DN150: 0.001; DN200: 0.01; DN250: 0.01; DN300: 0.01; DN350: 0.01; DN400: 0.01; DN450: 0.1; DN500: 0.1; DN600: 0.1.\n\nWait full bore DN125, DN150 row: 999999, 0.01. Yes. And DN200,250,300: 9999999, 0.01. DN350-600: 9999999, 0.1. Good.\n\nHmm, this is getting really long. Let me decide: include model_level for bore_diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class, indicating_range_minimum_value, verification_scale_interval. 9 entries × 30 values. I'll write it. It's long but complete. Actually, you know, let me trim: indicating_range and verification_scale_interval are already fully captured in matrix_tables 6 & 7 (grouped). Including both is duplication per instructions (they did both in example). I'll include all. Total JSON maybe ~35-40KB. Acceptable.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider — is there risk the response gets truncated? Possibly, but I'll aim for completeness while writing compactly (no unnecessary whitespace).\n\nLet me reconsider model_id choice once more. Shorter ids reduce size: \"FB DN32\" (8 chars) vs \"Full bore DN32\" (14). Over 270 value entries, saves ~1.6KB. Not decisive. Clarity wins: \"Full bore DN32\".\n\nWait, actually maybe include type in model_id since \"full bore\" maps to two type designations: model_id \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL DN32\" is 24 chars — long. I'll use \"Full bore DN32\" with label \"MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL (full bore), DN32\". Good.\n\nUnit ids: I'm fairly sure of \"u:degree_Celsius\" from the prompt example. For others leave null. m³/h symbol: \"m³/h\". MPa, bar, mm, V, m³.\n\nNow, certificate scheme: \"A\". oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nRevision history entries: revision as string: \"Initial\", \"1\"..\"6\". Schema says revision string e.g. \"0\". So \"Initial\" → \"0\"? It literally says \"Initial\". Rule: revision (string). I'll use \"0\" with changes \"-\" → changes: null? The table says \"-\". I'll put changes: \"Initial version\" — no, don't fabricate. changes: \"-\"? Or null. I'll use null for the initial row... hmm, changes \"-\" means none. I'll put \"changes\": null. Actually safer: \"changes\": \"Initial version\"? That's fabricating. Use null.\n\nDates ISO.\n\nNow let me also think about \"Documentation folder no. T10713-10\" — add to test_reports with role \"documentation_file\". Yes.\n\nAlso pressure-loss documentation references (10713/6-01 etc.) — I could add them as documentation_file entries too. They're sub-document references. The main doc folder T10713-10 covers them. I'll add just T10713-10 to avoid clutter... Actually the referenced docs 10713/1-01, /4-03, /6-01, /6-02, /12-01, /12-02, /12-03 are documentation items. Adding 7 more entries is noisy; I'll mention them in the config conditions (already done). Skip.\n\nNow assemble the JSON.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\",\n    \"revision\": \"6\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"4086814\",\n    \"page_total\": 7,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2026-08-10\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"the Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Certification Board\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Euromag International S.r.l.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Via Balla, 55\", \"35010 Villafranca Padovana (PD)\", \"Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Euromag International S.r.l.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Via Balla, 55\", \"35010 Villafranca Padovana (PD)\", \"Italy\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\", \"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meter, types MUT1000EL, MUT2200EL and MUT2300, with electronic converter MC406M or MC406AM. The measurement sensor can have a cylindrical measuring tube: full bore (MUT1000EL without flanges or MUT2200EL with flanges) or reduced bore (MUT2300).\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [ ... 30 entries ... ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"measuring sensor\",\n      \"type_designations\": [\"MUT1000EL\", \"MUT2200EL\", \"MUT2300\"],\n      \"characteristics\": {\n        \"measuring_tube\": \"cylindrical; full bore (MUT1000EL without flanges, MUT2200EL with flanges) or reduced bore (MUT2300)\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"electronic converter\",\n      \"type_designations\": [\"MC406M\", \"MC406AM\"],\n      \"characteristics\": {\n        \"configurations\": \"compact or remote; optional Bluetooth&RS485 and/or 4-20mA output module\"\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": { ... },\n    \"model_level\": [ ... 9 entries ... ],\n    \"config_level\": [ ... 3 entries ... ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [ ... 7 ... ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49-1\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"amendment\": null,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [ ... 10 ... ],\n  \"revision_history\": [ ... 7 ... ]\n}\n```\n\nAccuracy classes: numbers or strings? \"1 and 2\" → [1, 2] numbers fine. Or [\"1\",\"2\"]. I'll use numbers.\n\nRecommendation edition: 2013 int. Good.\n\nNow models array (30):\nFull bore: labels \"MUT1000EL/MUT2200EL (full bore), DN32\" etc.\nReduced: \"MUT2300 (reduced bore), DN50\".\n\nmodel_id: \"Full bore DN32\", ..., \"Reduced bore DN50\", ...\n\nHmm wait — should model_id reflect actual type designations for downstream matching? Let me include both: model_id \"Full bore DN32\", label \"MUT1000EL or MUT2200EL, DN32\". And reduced: model_id \"Reduced bore DN50\", label \"MUT2300, DN50\".\n\nNow model_level arrays. Let me write them all carefully.\n\nbore diameter (mm):\nFB: 32,40,50,65,80,100,125,150,200,250,300,350,400,450,500,600\nRB: 50,65,80,100,125,150,200,250,300,350,400,450,500,600\n\nQ1 (m³/h):\nFB: 0.125, 0.2, 0.315, 0.5, 0.8, 1.25, 2, 3.15, 5, 8, 12.5, 15.625, 20, 31.5, 39.375, 39.375\nRB: 0.2, 0.315, 0.5, 0.8, 0.8, 2, 3.15, 5, 8, 10, 12.5, 20, 25, 25\n\nQ2 (m³/h):\nFB: 0.2, 0.32, 0.504, 0.8, 1.28, 2, 3.2, 5.04, 8, 12.8, 20, 25, 32, 50.4, 63, 63\nRB: 0.32, 0.504, 0.8, 1.28, 1.28, 3.2, 5.04, 8, 12.8, 16, 20, 32, 40, 40\n\nQ3 (m³/h):\nFB: 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300, 6300, 6300\nRB: 40, 63, 100, 160, 160, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 1600, 2500, 4000, 4000, 4000\n\nQ4 (m³/h):\nFB: 31.25, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875, 7875, 7875\nRB: 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 200, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 5000, 5000, 5000\n\nRatio Q3/Q1:\nFB: 200 ×11 (DN32–DN300), then DN350: 160, DN400: 200, DN450: 200, DN500: 160, DN600: 160\nCheck: DN32 200, DN40 200, DN50 200, DN65 200, DN80 200, DN100 200, DN125 200, DN150 200, DN200 200, DN250 200, DN300 200, DN350 160, DN400 200, DN450 200, DN500 160, DN600 160. Yes.\nRB: 315 ×7 (DN50–DN200), DN250 200, DN300 200, DN350 160, DN400 200, DN450 200, DN500 160, DN600 160.\nCheck table: DN50 315, DN65 315, DN80 315, DN100 315, DN125 315, DN150 315, DN200 315, DN250 200, DN300 200, DN350 160, DN400 200, DN450 200, DN500 160, DN600 160. Yes.\n\naccuracy_class: FB all 2, RB all 1.\n\nAlright. This will be long, but let's write it. To save space I could omit \"unit_symbol\": null where absent? The schema example includes unit_symbol and unit_id in the strict shape. Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with that shape. Hmm — \"unit_id\" required too? The example shape shows both keys. For config/model-level values, the R137 example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit (unit at attribute level). So model_level value objects don't need unit per value — unit is at the attribute level (\"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"). The R137 model_level example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}...]}. Good — no per-value unit. And footnote_markers optional.\n\nFor type_level, each characteristic: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": ...}. I'll include unit_symbol when present, unit_id only where confident (°C → \"u:degree_Celsius\"). Omit unit_id elsewhere? The shape shows unit_id: null as part of the object. I'll include \"unit_id\": null explicitly for type_level entries to follow the strict shape. Hmm, that adds bulk; type_level has ~12 entries — fine, include.\n\nFor config_level values with units (voltage), include unit_symbol per value as shown in rule 5: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\". OK.\n\nMatrix tables: include column_units as in example. Rows as objects.\n\nLet me draft the pressure loss matrix table description: \"Pressure loss classes for reduced bore type MUT2300, sizes < DN250 (documentation 10713/4-03, 10713/12-02, 10713/12-03)\". Footnotes: none.\n\nSoftware tables: name, columns [\"software_version\",\"crc_checksum\"], rows.\n\nNow let me also double-check \"Accuracy class\" overall: recommendation.accuracy_classes [1,2]. Good.\n\nTest report roles — all \"type_evaluation_report\". The NMi-4086814-01 matches project number → likely the latest TER. Fine.\n\nLet me now write the final JSON. I'll be careful with JSON validity: no trailing commas, proper escaping of \"³\" (fine as UTF-8), \"&\" fine, \"Δ\" fine, \"≥\"/\"≤\"/\"°\" fine.\n\nOne more thought on the \"certificate.revision\" extra field — I'll include it; harmless and informative. Actually, hmm, strict schema adherence... The instructions say \"conforming to the layered schema\". Extra keys might break strict validators. But revision info is genuinely part of the document and revision_history captures it (revision \"6\", date matches date_issued). To be safe, I'll skip the extra \"revision\" key on certificate. Hmm. But then number loses \"revision 6\" info... revision_history has it. OK skip. Actually — many consumers would want the full number string. I could make number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-19.01\" and that's the canonical cert number; revision in history. Decided: omit extra key. Hmm, wait, actually let me reconsider once more... The example schema certificate fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I'll conform exactly. Revision appears in revision_history. Good.\n\nAlso \"scheme (A|B)\" — \"A\".\n\nPage_total: 7.\n\nNow write out all model_level entries.\n\nEntry template:\n{\"attribute\": \"bore_diameter_inlet_outlet\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"Full bore DN32\", \"value\": 32}, ...]}\n\nFull list of model ids:\nFB: \"Full bore DN32\",\"Full bore DN40\",\"Full bore DN50\",\"Full bore DN65\",\"Full bore DN80\",\"Full bore DN100\",\"Full bore DN125\",\"Full bore DN150\",\"Full bore DN200\",\"Full bore DN250\",\"Full bore DN300\",\"Full bore DN350\",\"Full bore DN400\",\"Full bore DN450\",\"Full bore DN500\",\"Full bore DN600\"\nRB: \"Reduced bore DN50\",\"Reduced bore DN65\",\"Reduced bore DN80\",\"Reduced bore DN100\",\"Reduced bore DN125\",\"Reduced bore DN150\",\"Reduced bore DN200\",\"Reduced bore DN250\",\"Reduced bore DN300\",\"Reduced bore DN350\",\"Reduced bore DN400\",\"Reduced bore DN450\",\"Reduced bore DN500\",\"Reduced bore DN600\"\n\nAttribute names:\n- bore_diameter_inlet_outlet (mm)\n- minimum_flow_rate_q1 (m³/h)\n- transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h)\n- permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h)\n- overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_q1 (null unit)\n- accuracy_class (null)\n- indicating_range_minimum_value (m³)\n- verification_scale_interval_minimum_resolution (m³)\n\nWrite them.\n\nNow matrix_tables.\n\nTable \"full_bore_general_characteristics\":\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\",\"diameter_inlet_outlet\",\"q1\",\"q2\",\"q3\",\"q4\",\"ratio_q3_q1\"]\ncolumn_units: [null,\"mm\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null]\nrows:\n{\"meter_size\":\"DN32\",\"diameter_inlet_outlet\":32,\"q1\":0.125,\"q2\":0.2,\"q3\":25,\"q4\":31.25,\"ratio_q3_q1\":200}\n{\"meter_size\":\"DN40\",\"diameter_inlet_outlet\":40,\"q1\"